% ============================================================================
% us-coins — what each US coin is worth, in CENTS (adj-facts-stdlib, MONEY).
% ============================================================================
% A money fact everyone learns at the register: "a nickel is five cents, a
% quarter is twenty-five." Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? coin_cents(quarter, $Cents)      % 25
%     ? coin_cents(nickel,  $Cents)      % 5
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `coin_cents(coin, cents)` carrying the table's citation, so the lookup above is
% the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the value WITH its source,
% on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains honestly on a token that is not
% a US coin (it never invents a value). The relation runs both ways: `?
% coin_cents($Coin, 5)` recalls the coin WORTH five cents — the nickel.
%
% Because the `cents` value is a NUMBER, a recalled value composes straight into
% arithmetic — the concrete bridge from RECALL to COMPUTE:
%
%     how many cents in three quarters?  3 × coin_cents(quarter) = 3 × 25 = 75
%
% (the U.S. Mint's own "Money Patterns" activity works exactly this sum: "If you
% have 3 quarters, you have seventy-five cents ($0.75).")
%
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% PROVENANCE — every value is byte-quoted from the U.S. Mint (nothing from memory)
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Each coin's cent value is copied VERBATIM from that coin's page in the U.S.
% Mint's Coin Classroom (kids.usmint.gov, "© 2025 United States Mint, U.S.
% Department of Treasury"). `trust authoritative` — the Mint is the agency that
% mints US circulating coins. The per-row quote and its exact URL:
%
%   penny   → 1    "The one-cent coin ceased circulating in 2025 after 232 years
%                    of production."          https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint/penny
%   nickel  → 5    "The nickel is the United States' five-cent coin."
%                                             https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint/nickel
%   dime    → 10   "The dime is the United States' 10-cent coin."
%                                             https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint/dime
%   quarter → 25   "In 1804, the Mint marked the quarter with "25c," meaning 25
%                    cents."                  https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint/quarter
%   half_dollar → 50  "The half dollar is the United States' 50-cent coin."
%                                             https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint/half-dollar
%   dollar  → 100  "The dollar is the United States' 100-cent coin. It takes 100
%                    pennies to equal a dollar!"
%                                             https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint/dollar
%
% ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope per table (per-row provenance is a
% documented future extension — ADJ-TABLES §6), so the `source`/`locator` below
% cite the Mint's Coin Classroom hub, whose "Circulating Coins" section
% enumerates exactly these six denominations; the per-row byte-quotes above pin
% each individual value to its own U.S. Mint page. (See ../README.md; ADJ-TABLES.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table coin_cents {
    columns coin, cents

    row (penny, 1)
    row (nickel, 5)
    row (dime, 10)
    row (quarter, 25)
    row (half_dollar, 50)
    row (dollar, 100)

    source "The denominations you'll see the most are the penny, nickel, dime, and quarter. The Mint makes half dollars and dollars for collecting, but you can still spend them."
    locator "https://kids.usmint.gov/about-the-mint"
    trust authoritative
}
